EN_B = [
("Organ Donation", "Each year, a large number of Indian patients lose their lives while waiting for a kidney, liver or heart transplant. The gap between the number of patients and the number of available organs is enormous. One person who donates organs after brain death can save as many as eight lives and improve many more through the donation of tissues like corneas and skin. Despite this, organ donation rates in India remain low. Lack of awareness, religious misconceptions and the emotional difficulty of taking such a decision at a time of grief are the main barriers. Hospitals need trained transplant coordinators who can counsel families gently and explain the process. A national registry now allows people to pledge their organs online, and green corridors are created on roads so that organs can be transported quickly between hospitals. Talking openly about organ donation within families is the most effective way to ensure that a person's wishes are respected."),
("The Indian Monsoon", "The southwest monsoon brings about seventy five percent of the yearly rain the country receives, during the four months from June. It usually reaches the coast of Kerala around the first of June and then advances northwards to cover the entire country by early July. The monsoon is driven by the difference in temperature between the land and the Indian Ocean. As the land heats up in summer, it creates an area of low pressure that pulls in moist winds from the sea. A good monsoon fills reservoirs, recharges groundwater and supports the sowing of kharif crops such as rice, maize, cotton and soybean. A weak or delayed monsoon, on the other hand, can lead to drought, lower farm output and higher food prices. The India Meteorological Department issues long range forecasts in April and updates them in June. Scientists are also studying how climate change is making rainfall more erratic, with long dry spells broken by very heavy downpours."),
("Ethics in Public Service", "Public servants hold positions of trust because they make decisions that affect the lives of millions of citizens. Honesty, impartiality and accountability are therefore the core values of public service. An officer must use public resources carefully, treat every citizen equally and avoid any conflict between personal interest and official duty. Ethical dilemmas often arise in daily work. For example, a junior officer may receive an order from a senior that seems unfair to the poor, or may face pressure to favour a powerful person. In such situations, rules provide some guidance, but personal integrity and courage are equally important. Transparent procedures, online services and the right to information have reduced the scope for corruption. Still, systems alone cannot guarantee good governance. Training programmes now include case studies and discussions on values, so that officers learn to think through difficult choices before they face them in real life."),
("Battery Recycling", "The rapid spread of mobile phones, laptops, electric scooters and solar systems has created a huge demand for lithium ion batteries. When these batteries reach the end of their life, they become a source of hazardous waste. If thrown into ordinary garbage, they can catch fire or leak toxic chemicals into soil and water. At the same time, they contain valuable metals such as lithium, cobalt, nickel and copper, which can be recovered and reused. Recycling therefore protects the environment and reduces the need for imports of these critical minerals. The government has notified rules under which every producer must take back and recycle the batteries it puts on the market. Several companies have set up plants that use mechanical and chemical processes to extract metals with high purity. A major challenge is that much of the collection is still done by the informal sector, often without safety equipment. Formalising this chain is essential for a clean and safe recycling industry."),
("Life in a Hill Village", "Life in a hill village moves at its own steady rhythm. Houses made of stone and wood are built on terraced slopes, and narrow footpaths connect one hamlet to another. Families grow wheat, potatoes, kidney beans and seasonal vegetables on small fields carved out of the mountainside. Most homes keep cows and goats, and women walk long distances to collect fodder and firewood. Winters are harsh, and heavy snowfall can cut off roads for days. In recent years, many young people have migrated to towns and cities in search of jobs, leaving behind elderly parents and empty houses. Some villages have tried to reverse this trend through homestay tourism, organic farming and the sale of local products like honey, herbs and woollen shawls. Better roads, mobile connectivity and health centres can make it possible for families to earn a decent living without leaving the mountains they call home."),
("Digital Public Infrastructure", "Over the last decade, India has built a set of digital platforms that are now known as digital public infrastructure. These include a unique digital identity for residents, a real time payment system that works through mobile phones, and a framework for sharing documents and data with consent. Because these platforms are open and interoperable, banks, government departments and private companies can build services on top of them. As a result, a farmer in a remote village can receive subsidy directly in a bank account, a street vendor can accept payments through a QR code, and a student can store certificates in a digital locker. Several countries have shown interest in adopting similar systems. Experts, however, caution that such platforms must be backed by strong data protection laws, reliable grievance redressal and options for people who are not comfortable with technology, so that nobody is excluded from essential services."),
("The Value of Sports in School", "Sports are often treated as an extra activity in many schools, squeezed into a single period at the end of the week. This approach overlooks the many benefits that physical activity brings to young people. Regular exercise strengthens the heart, muscles and bones and helps prevent obesity, which is rising among urban children. Team games teach cooperation, leadership and respect for rules. Losing a match gracefully and working harder for the next one builds resilience that is useful in every walk of life. Studies also show that students who play regularly tend to concentrate better in class and manage stress more effectively. To make sports a real part of education, schools need playgrounds, trained coaches and equipment, as well as a timetable that gives students daily time for physical activity. Parents, too, must understand that an hour on the field is not a waste of study time but an investment in health and character."),
("Wetlands and Their Role", "Wetlands are areas where land is covered by water for all or part of the year, such as marshes, lakes, swamps and river floodplains. They are among the most productive ecosystems on Earth. Wetlands store floodwater during heavy rain and release it slowly, reducing the risk of floods downstream. They recharge groundwater, filter pollutants and provide food and shelter to fish, birds and many other creatures. Millions of migratory birds travel thousands of kilometres every winter to Indian wetlands like Chilika, Bharatpur and Sultanpur. Local communities depend on wetlands for fishing, grazing and the collection of reeds and lotus stems. Unfortunately, many wetlands have been filled up for housing, roads and agriculture, or choked by sewage and plastic waste. India has designated a large number of sites under the Ramsar Convention, an international treaty for wetland protection. Mapping, legal protection and community participation are needed to save the remaining ones."),
("Generic Medicines", "Generic medicines contain the same active ingredient, in the same dose and form, as the branded medicines they replace. They are usually much cheaper because their makers do not spend large sums on research, advertising and brand promotion. India is often called the pharmacy of the world, as it supplies affordable generic drugs to many countries. Within the country, the government has opened thousands of special pharmacies that sell quality generic medicines at low prices. For families who spend heavily on treatment, especially for long term illnesses like diabetes and high blood pressure, this can mean significant savings. Some patients still hesitate to use generics because they believe that a cheaper medicine must be less effective. Doctors can help by writing the generic name of the drug in prescriptions and explaining the facts to patients. Strict quality testing by regulators is essential to maintain public trust in these medicines."),
("The Art of Listening", "Most people think of communication as speaking well, but good communication depends just as much on listening. Active listening means giving full attention to the speaker, understanding the message and responding thoughtfully. It requires us to put aside our phones, avoid interrupting and resist the temptation to prepare our reply while the other person is still talking. A good listener asks questions to clarify doubts and sometimes repeats the main point in his or her own words to show understanding. In the workplace, managers who listen carefully earn the trust of their teams and learn about problems before they become serious. In families, listening helps parents understand the worries of their children and strengthens relationships. Doctors who listen patiently are able to diagnose illness more accurately. Listening is a skill that can be practised and improved, and it often turns an ordinary conversation into a meaningful exchange of ideas."),
("Semiconductor Manufacturing", "Tiny semiconductor chips control almost every gadget we use today. They are found in mobile phones, computers, cars, medical equipment and even washing machines. For many years, India designed chips but depended almost entirely on imports for their manufacture. The shortage of chips during the pandemic showed how risky this dependence could be. The government has since announced a large incentive scheme to attract companies to set up fabrication units, as well as plants for assembling, testing and packaging chips. Building a chip factory is extremely costly and complex. It needs an uninterrupted supply of electricity, huge quantities of ultra pure water and thousands of highly skilled engineers. Several projects have now been approved in different states. Universities are also starting specialised courses to prepare the required workforce. If these efforts succeed, India could become an important part of the global electronics supply chain and reduce its import bill significantly."),
("Kitchen Gardens", "A kitchen garden is a small patch of land, a balcony or even a rooftop where a family grows vegetables, herbs and fruits for its own use. Such gardens have become popular in cities as people look for fresh and chemical free food. Tomatoes, chillies, spinach, coriander, mint and bottle gourd can be grown easily in pots or grow bags. Kitchen waste such as vegetable peels and tea leaves can be turned into compost, which enriches the soil and reduces the garbage sent to landfills. Gardening also has benefits beyond food. It provides gentle exercise, reduces stress and gives children a chance to learn where their food comes from. In rural areas, nutrition gardens promoted through government schemes and self help groups are helping families add more vegetables to their diet and save money. With a little planning, sunlight and regular watering, almost anyone can enjoy the satisfaction of eating what they have grown."),
("Judicial Review", "Under judicial review, a constitutional court can test the laws made by legislatures and the orders passed by governments, and can declare invalid any law or order that violates the Constitution. This power is exercised mainly by the Supreme Court and the High Courts. It acts as an important check that keeps every branch of government within its constitutional limits. Through judicial review, the courts have protected fundamental rights, struck down arbitrary rules and expanded the meaning of the right to life to include the right to a clean environment, education and privacy. The Supreme Court has also held that certain basic features of the Constitution, such as democracy, federalism and secularism, cannot be destroyed even by a constitutional amendment. Critics sometimes argue that courts should not enter areas of policy that belong to elected governments. A healthy democracy, therefore, depends on mutual respect and restraint among all its institutions."),
("Ports and Coastal Shipping", "India has a long coastline with major ports on both the eastern and western seas. For a large part of its history, coastal shipping was an important way of moving goods, but in modern times road and rail took over most of this traffic. Now there is renewed interest in using the sea and rivers for transport. Carrying heavy goods like coal, cement, fertilisers and steel by sea is cheaper, uses less fuel and causes less pollution than transport by road. New berths, better cargo handling equipment and faster customs clearance have reduced the time ships spend at ports. Projects to develop inland waterways on the Ganga and other rivers aim to connect the hinterland with the sea. Coastal economic zones are being planned near ports to attract industries. For these plans to succeed, ports must be linked with good roads and railway lines so that goods can reach their final destination smoothly."),
("The Joy of Volunteering", "Volunteering means offering one's time and skills for a social cause without expecting payment. It can take many forms, such as teaching children in a slum, helping at an animal shelter, cleaning a riverbank or supporting patients in a hospital. People often begin volunteering with the idea of helping others, but they soon discover that they gain a great deal themselves. They meet people from different backgrounds, learn new skills and develop a deeper understanding of social problems. Many young people find that volunteering helps them choose a career or adds valuable experience to their resume. Studies even suggest that people who volunteer regularly feel happier and less lonely. Organisations that work with volunteers must, however, give them proper training and meaningful tasks, so that their enthusiasm does not fade. Even a few hours a month, given consistently, can make a real difference to a community and to the volunteer as well."),
("Air Quality Index", "The Air Quality Index is a tool that tells people, in simple terms, how clean or polluted the air is on a particular day. It converts the concentrations of several pollutants, including fine particles, nitrogen dioxide, sulphur dioxide, ozone and carbon monoxide, into a single number. This number is then placed in one of six categories, ranging from good to severe, each with a colour code. When the index enters the poor or very poor range, people with asthma, heart disease and other health conditions are advised to reduce outdoor activity. In severe conditions, authorities may stop construction work, restrict the entry of trucks, close schools or ask offices to allow work from home. Monitoring stations are now installed in many cities, and real time readings are available on mobile apps. While the index helps people protect themselves, the real solution lies in cutting emissions from vehicles, industries, construction dust and the burning of crop residue."),
("Handloom Heritage", "India has a rich tradition of handloom weaving that has been passed down through generations. Each region has its own distinctive style, such as the Banarasi silk of Varanasi, the Kanjeevaram of Tamil Nadu, the Pochampally ikat of Telangana and the Muga silk of Assam. Weaving a single saree by hand can take several days or even weeks of careful work. Despite its beauty, the handloom sector faces serious difficulties. Cheap powerloom and machine made imitations sell at lower prices, and many weavers earn too little to support their families. As a result, young people are leaving the profession. To protect this heritage, the government observes National Handloom Day on 7 August, provides yarn at subsidised rates and promotes handloom products through exhibitions and online platforms. Geographical indication tags help genuine products stand out. Consumers, too, can play their part by choosing authentic handloom goods and paying a fair price for them."),
("The Census and Its Importance", "A census is the official counting of the population of a country, carried out at regular intervals. In India, the census has traditionally been conducted every ten years and is one of the largest administrative exercises in the world. Enumerators visit every household to record details such as age, sex, education, occupation, religion, language and housing conditions. This information is used to plan schools, hospitals, roads and welfare schemes, and to decide the allocation of funds between states. It also forms the basis for the delimitation of constituencies and the reservation of seats. The next census is expected to use mobile apps for data collection, which should make the process faster and reduce errors. Protecting the privacy of the information collected is a legal duty of the government. Accurate census data is essential, because policies based on outdated or incorrect figures can leave many people without the services they need."),
("Fighting Antibiotic Resistance", "Antibiotics are among the greatest discoveries of modern medicine. They have saved millions of lives by curing infections that were once deadly. However, the excessive and careless use of antibiotics has allowed many bacteria to become resistant to them. When this happens, common infections become difficult or even impossible to treat. Several factors contribute to this problem. Patients often take antibiotics for viral illnesses like colds, where they are useless, or stop the course midway once they feel better. Antibiotics are also sold without prescription in many places and are widely used in poultry and animal farming to speed up growth. Health authorities have launched campaigns urging people never to take antibiotics without a doctor's advice. Hospitals are improving infection control and tracking resistance patterns. Scientists, meanwhile, are searching for new drugs and alternative treatments. Responsible use by doctors, patients and farmers is essential to keep these life saving medicines effective."),
("Tourism in the Northeast", "The northeastern states of India are blessed with misty hills, roaring rivers, rich forests and a remarkable diversity of tribes, languages and festivals. Places like Tawang, Kaziranga, Shillong, Ziro, Majuli and Loktak Lake attract visitors who want to experience nature and culture away from crowded tourist spots. For a long time, poor connectivity limited the growth of tourism in the region. New airports, better highways and improved rail links are now making travel easier. Homestays run by local families give tourists a chance to enjoy traditional food, crafts and hospitality while providing income directly to villagers. Festivals such as the Hornbill Festival in Nagaland have become major attractions. Responsible tourism is especially important here, because the ecosystems are fragile and local traditions must be respected. Limiting plastic use, following local customs and supporting community based enterprises can ensure that tourism benefits the people of the region without harming their land."),
]
